title: 'pallet-assets: mirror balance changes as ERC-20 Transfer logs via AssetsCallback'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `AssetsCallback` gains infallible balance-change hooks (`issued`, `transferred`,
    `burned`, `deposited`, `withdrawn`) fired next to the matching pallet-assets events on
    every path: extrinsics, the `fungibles` traits (incl. XCM asset transactors) and the
    `Balanced` imbalance paths. The trait gains a third generic parameter `Balance`; any
    hand-written `impl AssetsCallback<Id, Acct>` must add it (implementations wired only as
    `CallbackHandle` need no change beyond a recompile). Genesis asset creation now fires
    `created` as well, so a `CallbackHandle` implementing it seeds genesis assets like
    runtime-created ones; a failure there panics genesis construction.

    On a runtime that wires a log-mirroring `CallbackHandle`, every balance-changing operation
    deposits an additional `pallet_revive::Event::ContractEmitted` right after the pallet-assets
    event, so per-extrinsic event count and ordering change for substrate-side consumers. Code
    asserting on the last emitted event (e.g. `assert_last_event`) must assert presence instead.

    `pallet_revive::Pallet::emit_contract_log_outside_frame` is exposed for runtime components that
    mirror non-contract activity as EVM logs from outside any contract call frame. The call tracer gains
    a matching `Tracing::log_event_outside_frame` hook so tracing a block that contains such a log
    no longer panics; the in-frame `log_event` (the `LOG` opcode) keeps its active-frame invariant.

    A new `Erc20TransferLogsCallback` callback in `pallet-assets-precompiles` mirrors those balance
    changes as canonical ERC-20 `Transfer` logs at the asset's precompile address, so plain
    substrate asset activity is visible to Ethereum log consumers. The `ERC20` precompile no
    longer emits its own `Transfer` log; wire `Erc20TransferLogsCallback` as the instance's
    `CallbackHandle` to keep precompile transfers logged. On Asset Hub Westend it is wired for the
    trust-backed and pool asset instances (inline-id) and for the foreign-assets instance
    (foreign-id, via `ForeignIdConfig` and the foreign asset-index map).

    Rollout to a live Asset Hub (e.g. Polkadot) needs care beyond wiring the callback:
      - Atomic cutover: removing the precompile self-emit and wiring the callback must ship in the
        same runtime upgrade, else a block range emits zero or duplicate `Transfer` logs.
      - Weights: the extrinsic-path mirror log must be benchmarked (`/cmd bench`) before production;
        the placeholder weight leaves it effectively unmetered.
      - Foreign asset-index mappings must exist before a mirror can resolve a token address. They
        are established by the creation callback, including for genesis-seeded assets; assets that
        predate the callback wiring need the index-map migration to backfill them.
      - Asset destruction has no mirror: pallet-assets can destroy an asset, but ERC-20 has no
        equivalent concept, so there is nothing to project `destroy_accounts` onto. Firing a burn
        log per holder instead would emit an unbounded number of events for a single destruction.
        Log consumers must drop a token's balances once the asset is destroyed, or an inline id
        later reused by `force_create` will inherit them.
crates:
- name: pallet-assets
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: minor
- name: pallet-assets-precompiles
  bump: major
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: major
